.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Bhabha, Heitler, Carlson, and Oppenheimer (1937) explained the cosmic-ray &quot;transition curve&quot; -- the number of shower particles first growing, then shrinking, with depth -- as a branching cascade: a single high-energy electron, positron, or photon alternately radiates a photon or materializes one into an electron-positron pair, each product repeating the process until individual energies fall low enough for ordinary ionization losses to take over. This example builds exactly this kind of branching cascade with simple_shower, checks that its total four-momentum is conserved from the single primary particle down to every final-state leaf, and shows the exponential growth in particle count with generation that gives the transition curve its characteristic rise.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/particle/detector_signatures/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_cascade_showers_thumb.gif
